The formula for the maximum number of spectral lines emitted when an electron returns from the $n^{	ext{th}}$ state to the ground state is $\dfrac{n(n-1)}{2}$.<br><br>Given that the maximum number of lines is $6$:<br><br>$\dfrac{n(n-1)}{2} = 6$<br><br>$n^2 - n - 12 = 0$<br><br>Solving this quadratic equation, we get $(n-4)(n+3) = 0$. Since $n$ must be positive, $n = 4$.<br><br>The highest energy photon is emitted during the transition with the largest energy gap, which is from $n = 4$ directly to the ground state ($n = 1$).<br><br>Using the Rydberg equation for wavenumber $ ar{
u}$:<br><br>$ ar{
u} = R_H \left( \dfrac{1}{n_1^2} - \dfrac{1}{n_2^2} ight)$<br><br>$ ar{
u} = R_H \left( \dfrac{1}{1^2} - \dfrac{1}{4^2} ight)$<br><br>$ ar{
u} = R_H \left( 1 - \dfrac{1}{16} ight) = \dfrac{15}{16} R_H$<br><br>Answer: $\dfrac{15}{16} R_H$

